{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791129974,"nonce":"3wrhux9+hByZ6aznHAejqs1OT8w=","message":"Fixed, with the approach from the ashui port.\n\nEach added piece now reaches `join_depth` into the body, where it is\nhidden: min(8, min(inner_w, inner_h)/2), capped so a piece cannot reach\nthrough a small shape. Any point near a join is then deep inside at\nleast one piece, so the union's interior distance measures to the\noutline rather than to the piece's own buried edge.\n\n- Concave flare boxes widen toward the body: tl/bl extend right, tr/br\n  extend left.\n- Bulge caps clip at base_y + join_depth instead of base_y, on the top\n  and bottom edges both.\n- Peak triangles move their base vertices along their own SIDES, with\n  spread = half_w * (h + join_depth) / h, so the apex and slopes are\n  untouched and only the buried base moves. Top and bottom both.\n- The scoop's hollow rect is unioned only when it has height. When\n  depth \u003c= half_w the disk fills the bowl and the rect is degenerate,\n  yet smax still pulled the top edge down across the whole scoop width.\n  Top and bottom both.\n\nThe bottom-edge mirrors had the same defect and are fixed with it,\nalthough only the top-edge cases were reported.\n\nVerified by rendering, not by reasoning:\nexamples/blinc_app_examples/examples/notch_border_capture.rs draws the\nfour shapes with a 2px border offscreen. Before, the border cut a chord\nacross the bulge's dome, a line across the peak's base, and a hard\nvertical bar where each flare meets the body. After, every outline\nfollows the silhouette, and the scoop is unchanged, as expected for a\nsubtraction.\n\nOne thing checked and dismissed: a faint patch remains just above the\ntop border near the flare. It measures identically before and after\n(mean 80.25 over the same strip) and does not vary with join_depth\nacross 2, 8 and 16, so it predates this change and is not caused by it.\nNot chased further here.\n\nAll 12 composed shaders still validate and cross-compile to HLSL\nthrough naga, which is what the CI DXC gate exercises.","files":null}]}